+ 1. Collision framework
In the end we make attack subtypes / type the attack data. A switch-case goes over all attack types.
Over time common patterns may appear, and attack types reduce to one type with parameters or
a small fixed set of types (for example, thrust (spear with direction), swing (rotation around an axis))

+ 2. Animations
Keep the states as they are

+ 3. Routines + Task -> custom IAsyncResult that works as a routine and keeps context

4. Collider editor
Circles, triangles, squares for now

META

1. Correct runtime changes of config values

2. State snapshot and input recording. For example, take a snapshot every minute and record the input
of the next minute. If a bug happens, it can be reproduced. Keep two or more instances
for edge cases like "the bug starts in the first minute but continues in the second"
Blocker: Jobs are built on C# async, and a state machine can't be serialized. Options: re-simulate
from a known point (fixed timestep + recorded input) or a custom language/VM for jobs.

3. Full debugging with rewind and maybe branching. Stop the game, rewind a few
frames, change input or config and run again. Requires the previous items

4. Job visibility
- Automatic job names: JobAsyncMethodBuilder.Start<TStateMachine> takes the method name from the
  compiler type name (<BladeAttack>d__3 -> BladeAttack), cached per type. No call-site annotations.
- Optional Job.Tag string for runtime details ("pw open", "stage 2").
- ImGui job tree: walk from the root job through subjobs and BlockJob, show name, tag, status
  and active timers.

5. Combat input
- Input buffering for Blade, Fire and Jump presses.
- Cooldowns per attack model.

6. Animation lifetime
Traces and fades are added to the animations pool without an owner job, while some animations are job
resources. Give every visual an owner (the job that made it, or a game-loop job for effects that outlive it)
and clean up the animation workarounds.
